                <h2>Why AI Copy Tends to Collide</h2>
<p>A recent phenomenon among teams running overseas advertising: ask AI to write 20 ad copies, and 10 end up as "twins." This isn't a tool limitation—it's caused by homogenized prompts and lack of post-processing. </p>
<p>One app development team tested this: using the same prompt across three different AI tools, each generating 15 copies, the repetition rate hit 47%. Your competitors are likely using the same approach.</p>
<p>This SOP breaks "AI generation → differentiation → launch" into 5 actionable steps. Operators can follow these to reduce copy collisions.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Redefine the Task—Don't Let AI Do "Fill-in-the-Blank"</h2>
<p>Most people's prompt habit looks like this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Write an ad for product XX, make it attractive, include a CTA, keep it under 20 words"</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This causes AI to output large amounts of synonymous replacements within a "safe zone."</p>
<p><strong>The correct approach: give AI a clear task framework and constraints:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Inefficient Writing</th>
<th>Efficient Writing</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Tone</td>
<td>"Energetic"</td>
<td>"Like a 25-year-old tech enthusiast recommending to a friend"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Structure</td>
<td>"Attractive opening"</td>
<td>"Open with a rhetorical question, give a statistic in the second sentence, CTA in the third"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Constraints</td>
<td>"Under 20 words"</td>
<td>"Exactly at Twitter character limit, CTA on a separate line"</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Key point:</strong> Generate only 3-5 copies per prompt—less quantity forces you to write clearer constraints.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Force-Inject Unique Brand/Product Elements</h2>
<p>AI's corpus is universal; it doesn't know your product's unique selling points.</p>
<p><strong>Mandatory three modules in prompts:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Core differentiation</strong> (within 15 characters): Write at the prompt's first line</li>
<li><strong>Real user pain points</strong>: Not generic phrases like "efficient and convenient," but specific scenarios like "a cross-border team discovered inventory mismatches on day three"</li>
<li><strong>Competitive angle</strong>: Even without naming competitors, specify "don't write like those 1-dollar flash sale ads"</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>Example Prompt:
Product: Inventory management SaaS
Differentiation: Supports multi-platform auto-sync
User pain point: Shopify and Amazon inventory data out of sync causing overselling

Prohibited: &quot;efficient,&quot; &quot;convenient,&quot; &quot;effortless,&quot; &quot;all-in-one&quot;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Force Differentiation Check After Batch Generation</h2>
<p>After generating 10 copies, don't rush to filter.</p>
<p><strong>Do this check immediately:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Split copies by sentence, examine each sentence separately</li>
<li>Count all opening lines: if more than 3 copies have the same structure, delete 2</li>
<li>Check CTAs: ensure at least 3 different expressions (question/command/number-driven)</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Tool assistance:</strong> Run copies through a similarity detection tool—keep only one copy if similarity exceeds 60%.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Two Key Human Intervention Points</h2>
<p>AI handles mass production; humans handle optimization. Intervention shouldn't wait until the end—it should happen in two stages:</p>
<p><strong>Point A - During generation (every 5 copies):</strong></p>
<p>Pause AI output for a quick review. If direction goes off track (e.g., all emphasizing price over features), immediately adjust the prompt and regenerate. Don't wait until all are finished.</p>
<p><strong>Point B - After filtering:</strong></p>
<p>From the selected 5-8 copies, force-add a "localization hook."</p>
<p>For example:</p>
<ul>
<li>North America: Add holiday/event elements</li>
<li>Southeast Asia: Add local slang or emojis</li>
<li>Middle East: Be mindful of religious/cultural sensitivities</li>
</ul>
<p>AI can't do this step well—human judgment is required.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Small-Scale Testing and Iteration Before Launch</h2>
<p>Copy differentiation effects ultimately need data validation.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended workflow:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>From each AI generation round, keep 2-3 most differentiated copies</li>
<li>Run A/B tests in small-budget ad groups (suggest $50-100 per group)</li>
<li>After 20 conversion events, compare CTR and CVR</li>
<li>Document successful copy prompt templates for reuse; analyze reasons for poor performers</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Iteration cadence:</strong> Run at least one round weekly—your copy library will gradually accumulate differentiated expressions that work for your product.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Does the prompt include clear differentiation descriptions?</li>
<li>[ ] Are tone/structure constraints specific to "who says it" rather than "how to say it"?</li>
<li>[ ] Is there similarity filtering after batch generation?</li>
<li>[ ] Were localization hooks added during filtering?</li>
<li>[ ] Is there small-budget testing before launch?</li>
</ul>
<p>You don't need all 5 steps to start—begin with Step 1 (revising prompts), and within two weeks, you'll see a significant drop in copy repetition.</p>
